Borrowing and Lifetimes

Rust's borrow checker ensures that references do not outlive the data they point to. A struct that holds a reference must be annotated with a lifetime parameter so the compiler can verify this at compile time, with zero runtime cost.

```rust
struct Excerpt<'a> {
    part: &'a str,
}

impl<'a> Excerpt<'a> {
    fn announce_and_return_part(&self, announcement: &str) -> &str {
        println!("Attention please: {}", announcement);
        self.part
    }
}

fn main() {
    let novel = String::from("Call me Ishmael. Some years ago...");
    let first_sentence = novel.split('.').next().expect("Could not find a '.'");
    let excerpt = Excerpt { part: first_sentence };
    println!("{}", excerpt.announce_and_return_part("here is an excerpt"));
}
```

Because `Excerpt` borrows from `novel`, the compiler will reject any code that lets `novel` go out of scope while an `Excerpt` referencing it is still alive. This prevents the classic dangling pointer bug found in languages like C and C++ without needing a garbage collector.
